(
  'GST Registration: Who Must Register and Complete Step-by-Step Process',
  'Find out if your business is liable for GST registration, what documents you need, and how to complete the registration process in 7 working days.',
  'GST registration is mandatory for businesses meeting the turnover threshold or operating in specific categories. Understanding who needs to register and how to do it correctly is the first step to GST compliance.\n\n## Who Must Register for GST?\n\n### Mandatory Registration (Regardless of Turnover)\n- Inter-state suppliers of goods or services\n- E-commerce operators and suppliers on e-commerce platforms\n- Casual taxable persons\n- Non-resident taxable persons\n- Those paying tax under reverse charge mechanism\n- Input Service Distributors (ISD)\n\n### Turnover-Based Registration\n| Category | Threshold |\n|----------|-----------|\n| Regular businesses (services) | ₹20 lakh |\n| Regular businesses (goods) | ₹40 lakh |\n| Special category states | ₹10 lakh |\n\n## Documents Required for GST Registration\n\n**For Proprietorship:**\n- PAN card of owner\n- Aadhaar card\n- Passport size photo\n- Proof of business address\n- Bank account details (cancelled cheque/bank statement)\n\n**Additional for Companies/LLPs:**\n- Certificate of incorporation\n- MOA/AOA or LLP agreement\n- PAN of all directors/partners\n- Board resolution authorizing signatory\n\n## Step-by-Step Registration Process\n\n**Step 1:** Visit gstn.gov.in → Services → Registration → New Registration\n\n**Step 2:** Fill Part A of Form REG-01:\n- Select taxpayer type\n- Enter PAN, mobile, email\n- Receive and validate OTP\n\n**Step 3:** Fill Part B with detailed business information:\n- Business name, address, promoter details\n- Nature of business activity\n- HSN/SAC codes for your products/services\n- Upload all required documents\n\n**Step 4:** Submit application using DSC or EVC\n\n**Step 5:** Receive ARN (Application Reference Number)\n\n**Step 6:** GST officer verifies within 7 working days. If documents are satisfactory, GSTIN is issued.\n\n## Post-Registration Compliance\n\nOnce registered:\n- Display GSTIN prominently at your place of business\n- Mention GSTIN on all invoices\n- Start filing returns from the month of registration\n- Issue proper GST invoices for all taxable supplies\n\n## Consequences of Not Registering\n\n- Penalty of 100% of tax due or ₹10,000 (whichever is higher)\n- Entire turnover treated as tax evaded\n- Cannot charge GST from customers or claim ITC',
  'GST', 'Priya Sharma', '✅', '6 min read',
  'GST Registration Process 2025: Who Must Register & Required Documents | GST Tax Wale',
  'Complete guide to GST registration in India. Find out who needs to register, required documents, and the step-by-step registration process.',
  'GST registration, GSTIN, GST registration documents, GST threshold, GST portal',
  1, NOW()
),
(
  'Section 80C Deductions: 15 Best Investment Options to Save Tax in 2025',
  'Maximize your Section 80C deductions up to ₹1.5 lakh. Compare PPF, ELSS, LIC, NPS, EPF, and other investment options to choose the best tax-saving instrument.',
  'Section 80C of the Income Tax Act is the most popular tax-saving provision, allowing you to reduce your taxable income by up to ₹1.5 lakh per year. Here is a comprehensive comparison of all eligible investments.\n\n## Section 80C: Maximum Deduction ₹1,50,000\n\n### Best Section 80C Investments Compared\n\n| Investment | Lock-in | Returns | Risk |\n|-----------|---------|---------|------|\n| PPF | 15 years | 7.1% (tax-free) | Zero |\n| ELSS Mutual Fund | 3 years | 12-15% (taxable) | Medium-High |\n| EPF | Until retirement | 8.25% (tax-free) | Zero |\n| NSC | 5 years | 7.7% | Zero |\n| 5-Year Tax Saver FD | 5 years | 6-7% | Zero |\n| Sukanya Samriddhi | Until girl turns 21 | 8.2% | Zero |\n| Senior Citizen Savings Scheme | 5 years | 8.2% | Zero |\n| Life Insurance Premium | Policy term | Depends | Low |\n| NPS (Tier 1) | Until age 60 | 10-12% | Medium |\n\n## Deep Dive: Top 5 Section 80C Options\n\n### 1. ELSS (Equity Linked Saving Scheme) — Best for Growth\n- Shortest lock-in (3 years)\n- Highest return potential (equity exposure)\n- Long-term capital gains taxed @10% above ₹1 lakh\n- Ideal for investors with 5+ year horizon\n\n### 2. PPF (Public Provident Fund) — Best for Safety\n- Government-backed, fully safe\n- Tax-free interest (EEE status)\n- Good for conservative investors\n- Can be extended in 5-year blocks after 15 years\n\n### 3. EPF (Employees Provident Fund) — Best for Salaried\n- Automatic 12% salary contribution\n- Employer contributes 12% (8.33% to EPS)\n- Tax-free on withdrawal after 5 years of service\n\n### 4. NPS (National Pension System) — Best for Retirement\n- Additional ₹50,000 deduction under Section 80CCD(1B)\n- Combined 80C + 80CCD(1B) = ₹2 lakh total saving\n- Market-linked returns\n\n### 5. Sukanya Samriddhi Yojana — Best for Girl Child\n- Highest interest rate (8.2%) among small savings\n- Fully tax-free (EEE)\n- Only for girls below 10 years\n\n## Beyond 80C: Other Tax-Saving Sections\n\n| Section | Deduction | Limit |\n|---------|-----------|-------|\n| 80D | Medical Insurance | ₹25,000 (₹50,000 for seniors) |\n| 80E | Education Loan Interest | No limit |\n| 80G | Donations | 50-100% of donation |\n| 24(b) | Home Loan Interest | ₹2,00,000 |\n| 80TTA | Bank Interest | ₹10,000 |\n| 80TTB | Senior Citizen Interest | ₹50,000 |\n\n**Pro Tip:** Invest early in the financial year. Last-minute investments reduce your monthly investable surplus and may lead to suboptimal investment choices.',
  'Income Tax', 'CA Mahendra Kumar', '💰', '9 min read',
  'Section 80C Tax Saving Investments 2025: PPF, ELSS, LIC Comparison | GST Tax Wale',
  'Compare all Section 80C tax-saving investments for 2025. PPF vs ELSS vs LIC vs NPS - find the best option to save ₹1.5 lakh in taxes.',
  'Section 80C, tax saving investments, PPF, ELSS, LIC, NPS, EPF, tax deductions',
  1, NOW()
